WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.378 --> 00:00:02.104
- Today we're gonna talk about the biggest

00:00:02.104 --> 00:00:03.904
time wasters in law school

00:00:03.904 --> 00:00:06.342
and how to avoid them for better grades.

00:00:06.342 --> 00:00:09.867
So stick around and get
an edge in law school.

00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:12.867
(light piano notes)

00:00:14.398 --> 00:00:16.580
Okay, time waster number one.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.171
This is the one that most students lose

00:00:18.171 --> 00:00:20.116
the most amount of time on.

00:00:20.116 --> 00:00:22.067
And those are case briefs.

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.724
These things are huge wastes of time.

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:27.571
These are the multi colored bastards

00:00:27.571 --> 00:00:29.555
that will suck up all of your time

00:00:29.555 --> 00:00:31.651
in your first semester in law school.

00:00:31.651 --> 00:00:33.666
You're summarizing all of the cases

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:35.835
that your law professors provides to you.

00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:37.995
All because you might've seen it on TV

00:00:37.995 --> 00:00:39.308
or on Legally Blonde.

00:00:39.308 --> 00:00:41.108
These are the biggest wastes of time.

00:00:41.108 --> 00:00:42.971
We have a whole video devoted to why

00:00:42.971 --> 00:00:45.003
case briefs are a bad idea.

00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:47.340
But the bottom line is that you just can't

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.595
use the information
that you're summarizing

00:00:49.595 --> 00:00:52.787
out of your law school
cases on your final exam.

00:00:52.787 --> 00:00:54.766
Don't waste your time on case briefs,

00:00:54.766 --> 00:00:56.787
there are better ways
of reading your cases.

00:00:56.787 --> 00:00:59.323
You can do it faster, you
can do it more efficiently,

00:00:59.323 --> 00:01:01.707
in fact, we have an entire e-book devoted

00:01:01.707 --> 00:01:03.811
specifically to how to read your cases.

00:01:03.811 --> 00:01:05.001
We give it away for free.

00:01:05.001 --> 00:01:06.243
You can get the link below.

00:01:06.243 --> 00:01:07.931
Time waster number two,

00:01:07.931 --> 00:01:11.564
preparing for in class cold calling.

00:01:11.564 --> 00:01:14.068
Law school is known for
the Socratic method.

00:01:14.068 --> 00:01:16.260
In other words, cold calling on students

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.895
to describe their cases or
to explain different nuances

00:01:20.895 --> 00:01:23.407
of different legal situations.

00:01:23.407 --> 00:01:26.223
For many people, this is terrifying.

00:01:26.223 --> 00:01:28.023
But, as terrifying as it is,

00:01:28.023 --> 00:01:30.125
it's also completely useless.

00:01:30.125 --> 00:01:32.470
There is almost a 0%
chance that you're going

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:35.733
to use what another
student has said in class

00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:37.071
on your final.

00:01:37.071 --> 00:01:38.831
You should prepare for class in the sense

00:01:38.831 --> 00:01:40.711
that you want to try and get a handle

00:01:40.711 --> 00:01:42.784
on the law that's going to be discussed.

00:01:42.784 --> 00:01:45.519
But it's just not worth
the diminishing returns

00:01:45.519 --> 00:01:48.903
of preparing for the off
chance that you might get

00:01:48.903 --> 00:01:50.511
cold called in class.

00:01:50.511 --> 00:01:52.277
Time waster number three,

00:01:52.277 --> 00:01:55.359
trying to impress your professor.

00:01:55.359 --> 00:01:58.335
Unlike college,
participation rarely counts

00:01:58.335 --> 00:02:00.130
for anything in law school.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.981
So don't go into class trying
to impress your professor.

00:02:02.981 --> 00:02:04.583
Don't go into office hours thinking that

00:02:04.583 --> 00:02:07.856
by discussing the cases
with your professor

00:02:07.856 --> 00:02:09.034
you're going to impress them

00:02:09.034 --> 00:02:10.695
and be able to talk up your grade.

00:02:10.695 --> 00:02:11.822
It's just not gonna happen.

00:02:11.822 --> 00:02:13.080
If you do go into office hours,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.520
do it for yourself.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.047
Try to understand
something that you weren't

00:02:17.047 --> 00:02:18.637
able to understand before.

00:02:18.637 --> 00:02:20.231
But don't go into office hours thinking

00:02:20.231 --> 00:02:22.056
that you're going to impress the professor

00:02:22.056 --> 00:02:23.908
and therefore get a better grade.

00:02:23.908 --> 00:02:26.381
Be wary of showing off in class.

00:02:26.381 --> 00:02:29.416
It's hard to listen when you're talking.

00:02:29.416 --> 00:02:31.861
Steven Covey said, "Most
people do not listen

00:02:31.861 --> 00:02:33.661
"with the intent to understand;

00:02:33.661 --> 00:02:35.975
"they listen with the intent to reply."

00:02:35.975 --> 00:02:38.448
And I see that in law school all the time.

00:02:38.448 --> 00:02:39.663
If your mouth is open,

00:02:39.663 --> 00:02:41.240
your ears are closed.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.249
So don't try to impress your professor.

00:02:43.249 --> 00:02:45.143
By the same token, another time waster

00:02:45.143 --> 00:02:47.070
is trying to impress your friends.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.770
A general rule in law
school, don't be a douche.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.207
And don't be a gunner.

00:02:51.207 --> 00:02:53.768
Gunners tend to do poorly in class.

00:02:53.768 --> 00:02:55.981
And that's because when you're talking,

00:02:55.981 --> 00:02:57.152
you're not listening.

00:02:57.152 --> 00:02:58.975
So it's better to listen in class.

00:02:58.975 --> 00:03:00.512
Don't try to impress your friends,

00:03:00.512 --> 00:03:02.639
don't try to impress
them in a study group,

00:03:02.639 --> 00:03:05.447
just listen, you'll be better off for it.

00:03:05.447 --> 00:03:08.206
And that brings me to
time waster number five.

00:03:08.206 --> 00:03:11.231
Which is trying to memorize
the detail of cases.

00:03:11.231 --> 00:03:14.087
Other than just the rule or the facts.

00:03:14.087 --> 00:03:17.134
We can spend an infinite amount of time

00:03:17.134 --> 00:03:20.359
trying to memorize every
single nuance of the case.

00:03:20.359 --> 00:03:21.231
But for the most part,

00:03:21.231 --> 00:03:23.671
your professor is going to
explain the most important

00:03:23.671 --> 00:03:25.991
parts of that case.

00:03:25.991 --> 00:03:28.140
There's no point in
trying to memorize every

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.941
single detail outside of
what your professor provides.

00:03:31.941 --> 00:03:33.644
So listen when your professor is talking

00:03:33.644 --> 00:03:34.973
about the cases.

00:03:34.973 --> 00:03:37.812
And certainly pay attention
to the facts of the case,

00:03:37.812 --> 00:03:39.994
and the bright line
rule if the case happens

00:03:39.994 --> 00:03:41.205
to provide that.

00:03:41.205 --> 00:03:43.830
But other than that,
similar to case briefing,

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:44.957
it's not worth your time

00:03:44.957 --> 00:03:48.742
in trying to memorize every
single portion of the cases

00:03:48.742 --> 00:03:51.833
you discuss or the history of the law

00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:53.423
or other legals aspects that you'll

00:03:53.423 --> 00:03:55.407
find in commercial outlines.

00:03:55.407 --> 00:03:58.647
Which brings me to time waster number six.

00:03:58.647 --> 00:04:02.253
Which is, looking for the right answer.

00:04:02.253 --> 00:04:06.009
In law school, you have
to be okay with ambiguity.

00:04:06.009 --> 00:04:08.676
Everything is one big gray area.

00:04:09.698 --> 00:04:12.319
In fact, in real life,
the cases that go to trial

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:15.129
are the ones that could go either way.

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:17.013
If a case is an easy one,

00:04:17.013 --> 00:04:18.842
then it will settle beforehand.

00:04:18.842 --> 00:04:21.002
And in law school you're going to be faced

00:04:21.002 --> 00:04:24.829
with ambiguous situation
after ambiguous situation.

00:04:24.829 --> 00:04:26.661
You have to come to grips with the idea

00:04:26.661 --> 00:04:29.868
that there may not be a right answer.

00:04:29.868 --> 00:04:33.087
You may have to go with
the least bad answer.

00:04:33.087 --> 00:04:36.555
Or you may have to deal with
differing shades of gray.

00:04:36.555 --> 00:04:38.906
Either way, you can't waste your time

00:04:38.906 --> 00:04:41.725
by trying to find the right answer.

00:04:41.725 --> 00:04:44.908
Metaphysics has no place in law school.

00:04:44.908 --> 00:04:46.404
Be comfortable with uncertainty

00:04:46.404 --> 00:04:47.561
and it will be your friend

00:04:47.561 --> 00:04:49.477
and you'll actually be able
to use it to great effect

00:04:49.477 --> 00:04:51.123
in law school exams.

00:04:51.123 --> 00:04:53.418
So if you can avoid
those six time wasters,

00:04:53.418 --> 00:04:55.403
you're gonna be off to a great start,

00:04:55.403 --> 00:04:59.139
and you're gonna have an
edge on everybody else.

